# Proof

**Claim:** Yes. Every $\mathbb{Q}$-character of $G = \operatorname{Res}_{K/\mathbb{Q}}(\mathbb{G}_m)$ is of the form $g \mapsto \det(g)^k$ for some $k \in \mathbb{Z}$, which on $G_{\mathbb{Q}} = K^*$ is $x \mapsto N_{\mathbb{Q}}^K(x^k)$.

## Setup

Let $K/\mathbb{Q}$ be a number field of degree $n$. The multiplicative group $K^*$ embeds into $GL_n(\mathbb{Q})$ via the left-regular representation: each $x \in K^*$ acts on the $n$-dimensional $\mathbb{Q}$-vector space $K$ by multiplication. The Zariski closure of the image is the $\mathbb{Q}$-algebraic group $G = \operatorname{Res}_{K/\mathbb{Q}}(\mathbb{G}_m)$, the Weil restriction of $\mathbb{G}_m$ from $K$ to $\mathbb{Q}$.

## Step 1: The character group over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$

Over a separable closure, $K \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \bar{\mathbb{Q}} \cong \prod_{\sigma} \bar{\mathbb{Q}}$, where $\sigma$ ranges over the $n$ embeddings $K \hookrightarrow \bar{\mathbb{Q}}$. Consequently:

$$G_{\bar{\mathbb{Q}}} \cong \prod_{\sigma} \mathbb{G}_m = (\mathbb{G}_m)^n,$$

with coordinates indexed by the embeddings $\sigma$. The character group of $(\mathbb{G}_m)^n$ over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ is $\mathbb{Z}^n$: a character corresponding to $(a_\sigma)_\sigma \in \mathbb{Z}^n$ is

$$\chi_a\bigl((z_\sigma)_\sigma\bigr) = \prod_\sigma z_\sigma^{a_\sigma}.$$

## Step 2: Galois action on characters

The isomorphism $\bar{\mathbb{Q}} \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} K \xrightarrow{\sim} \prod_\sigma \bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ sends $a \otimes x \mapsto (a\,\sigma(x))_\sigma$. The natural Galois action $\tau \cdot (a \otimes x) = \tau(a) \otimes x$ translates to the action on $(\bar{\mathbb{Q}}^*)^n$:

$$\tau \cdot (z_\sigma)_\sigma = \bigl(\tau(z_{\tau^{-1}\circ\sigma})\bigr)_\sigma.$$

The induced action on the character group $\mathbb{Z}^n$ is computed via $(\tau \cdot \chi)(g) = \tau\bigl(\chi(\tau^{-1}\cdot g)\bigr)$. A direct calculation gives:

$$(\tau \cdot a)_\rho = a_{\tau^{-1}\circ\rho},$$

i.e., the Galois group $\operatorname{Gal}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ permutes the coordinates of $\mathbb{Z}^n$ according to its action on the embeddings $\sigma$.

## Step 3: $\mathbb{Q}$-characters are Galois-invariant

A character $\chi_a$ is defined over $\mathbb{Q}$ if and only if it is fixed by the Galois action, i.e., $\tau \cdot a = a$ for all $\tau \in \operatorname{Gal}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$. This requires:

$$a_\rho = a_{\tau^{-1}\circ\rho} \quad \text{for all } \tau, \rho.$$

The Galois group acts **transitively** on the set of embeddings $K \hookrightarrow \bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ (these embeddings are in bijection with the coset space $\operatorname{Gal}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}) / \operatorname{Gal}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}}/K)$, on which the Galois group acts transitively). Therefore, Galois-invariance forces all coordinates to be equal:

$$a_\sigma = k \quad \text{for all } \sigma, \text{ for some } k \in \mathbb{Z}.$$

## Step 4: Identification with powers of the determinant

The resulting $\mathbb{Q}$-character is:

$$\chi_k\bigl((z_\sigma)_\sigma\bigr) = \prod_\sigma z_\sigma^k.$$

On the rational points $G_{\mathbb{Q}} = K^*$, an element $x \in K^*$ maps to $(\sigma(x))_\sigma$, so:

$$\chi_k(x) = \prod_\sigma \sigma(x)^k = \left(\prod_\sigma \sigma(x)\right)^k = N_{\mathbb{Q}}^K(x)^k = N_{\mathbb{Q}}^K(x^k).$$

Finally, the determinant of $x$ acting by multiplication on $K$ (as a $\mathbb{Q}$-vector space) equals the field norm:

$$\det(g) = N_{\mathbb{Q}}^K(x),$$

since the eigenvalues of the multiplication-by-$x$ map (over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$) are exactly $\{\sigma(x)\}_\sigma$. Hence:

$$\chi_k(g) = \det(g)^k.$$

## Conclusion

The group of $\mathbb{Q}$-characters of $G = \operatorname{Res}_{K/\mathbb{Q}}(\mathbb{G}_m)$ is isomorphic to $\mathbb{Z}$, generated by the determinant character $g \mapsto \det(g)$. Every $\mathbb{Q}$-character is of the form $g \mapsto \det(g)^k$ for a unique $k \in \mathbb{Z}$, which on $K^*$ is $x \mapsto N_{\mathbb{Q}}^K(x^k)$.

